-*   **Strengths:** Reduces quantization error for boundary effects by not penalizing undefined data [S1, unofficial]. Maintains convergence properties of standard $k$-means [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires explicit handling of masks. Performance may degrade if a large portion of data is undefined, leading to unstable centroid updates [G].
-*   **When to use:** When compressing data with irregular domains or boundaries where standard VQ would introduce artifacts at the edges [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K \cdot D \cdot T)$, where $T$ is the number of iterations. The mask operations add a small constant overhead.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D + K \cdot D)$ for storing vectors and centroids.
+- **Strengths:** Reduces quantization error for valid data by ignoring undefined components. Preserves convergence properties of k-means.
+- **Limitations:** Requires mask information. Performance may degrade if many components are undefined.
+- **When to use:** When dealing with sparse volumes or domains with irregular boundaries.
+- **When not to use:** When all data components are defined (standard VQ is simpler).
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in the context of compressed random-access trees for spatially coherent data [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the issue that subtrees in a mipmap tree may extend beyond the defined domain, resulting in residual blocks with undefined values [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the known property that $k$-means can be extended to partially defined data while preserving convergence [S1, unofficial]. It is used in conjunction with brood-based vector quantization to compress 3D data [S1, unofficial].
+This method is an extension of k-means clustering to partially defined data, based on the property that k-means can handle missing values by ignoring them during distance and mean calculations [LFWV03, cited in S1, unofficial]. It was applied to volume rendering compression in "Compressed Random-Access Trees for Spatially Coherent Data" [S1, unofficial].
 
